1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,820
BETH: Oh good morning. You must be James. I'm Beth Cartwright - please call me Beth.

2
00:00:07,820 --> 00:00:09,400
JAMES: Thank you.

3
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:12,420
BETH: Now as this is your first tutorial

4
00:00:12,420 --> 00:00:15,285
since you started on the Scandinavian Studies course,

5
00:00:15,285 --> 00:00:21,170
I'd like to find out something about you. Why did you decide to take this course?

6
00:00:21,170 --> 00:00:25,850
JAMES: Well, my mother is Danish, and although we always lived in England,

7
00:00:25,850 --> 00:00:31,970
she used to talk about her home a lot, and that made me want to visit Denmark.

8
00:00:31,970 --> 00:00:36,560
We hardly ever did, though - my mother usually went on her own.

9
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:42,380
But whenever her relations or friends were in England they always came to see us.

10
00:00:42,380 --> 00:00:46,920
BETH: I see. So I assume you already speak Danish

11
00:00:47,060 --> 00:00:49,330
one of the languages you'll be studying.

12
00:00:49,330 --> 00:00:55,190
JAMES: I can get by when I talk to people, though I'm not terribly accurate.

13
00:00:55,190 --> 00:01:00,508
BETH: Now you probably know that you'll spend the third year of the course abroad.

14
00:01:00,508 --> 00:01:02,990
Have you had any thoughts about that?

15
00:01:02,990 --> 00:01:05,280
JAMES: I'm really looking forward to it

16
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:08,717
And although Denmark seems the obvious place to go,

17
00:01:08,717 --> 00:01:13,370
because of my family connections, I'd love to spend the time in Iceland.

18
00:01:13,370 --> 00:01:15,979
BETH: Oh, I'm sure it can be arranged.

19
00:01:15,979 --> 00:01:19,060
Do you have any plans for when you graduate

20
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:22,500
A lot of students go on to take a master's degree.

21
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:28,041
JAMES: I think the four years of the undergraduate course will be enough for me.

22
00:01:28,041 --> 00:01:34,690
I'm interested in journalism and I quite like the idea of moving to Scandinavia and writing for magazines.

23
00:01:34,690 --> 00:01:41,080
I'd find that more creative than translating, which I suppose most graduates do.

24
00:01:41,080 --> 00:01:46,930
BETH: OK. Now how are you finding the courses you're taking this term, James?

25
00:01:46,930 --> 00:01:50,990
JAMES: Well, I'm really enjoying the one on Swedish cinema.

26
00:01:50,990 --> 00:01:52,968
BETH: That'll continue next term,

27
00:01:52,968 --> 00:02:00,090
but the one on Scandinavian literature that's running at the moment will be replaced by more specialised courses.

28
00:02:00,090 --> 00:02:04,848
Oh, and by the way, if you're interested in watching Danish television programmes -

29
00:02:04,848 --> 00:02:09,240
there's going to be a course on that the term after next.

30
00:02:09,240 --> 00:02:11,080
JAMES: That sounds good.

31
00:02:11,080 --> 00:02:16,540
BETH: Have you started thinking about the literature paper that you have to write in the next few weeks?

32
00:02:16,540 --> 00:02:22,220
JAMES: Yes, my first choice would be to do something on the Icelandic sagas.

33
00:02:22,220 --> 00:02:27,277
BETH: Hmm. The trouble with that is that a lot of people choose that topic,

34
00:02:27,277 --> 00:02:31,660
and it can be difficult to get hold of the books you'll need.

35
00:02:31,660 --> 00:02:34,410
Why not leave that for another time?

36
00:02:34,410 --> 00:02:35,530
JAMES: Right.

37
00:02:35,530 --> 00:02:40,190
BETH: You might find modern novels or 19th century playwrights interesting.

38
00:02:40,190 --> 00:02:46,440
JAMES: I've read or seen several plays in translation, so that would be a good idea.

39
00:02:46,440 --> 00:02:50,170
BETH: Fine. I'll put you down for that topic.

40
00:02:50,170 --> 00:02:54,460
JAMES: Right. So what would you advise me to aim at in the paper?

41
00:02:54,460 --> 00:02:57,680
BETH: First I suggest you avoid taking one writer

42
00:02:57,680 --> 00:03:00,090
and going into a great deal of detail.

43
00:03:00,090 --> 00:03:02,079
That approach certainly has its place,

44
00:03:02,079 --> 00:03:08,707
but I think you first need to get an understanding of the literature in the context of the society in

45
00:03:08,707 --> 00:03:14,010
which it was produced - who it was written for, how it was published, and so on.

46
00:03:14,010 --> 00:03:19,240
I also think that's more fruitful than placing it within the history of the genre.

47
00:03:19,240 --> 00:03:22,060
JAMES: OK, that sounds reasonable.

48
00:03:22,060 --> 00:03:27,330
JAMES: Could I ask for some advice about writing the paper I'm working on about the Vikings?

49
00:03:27,330 --> 00:03:31,050
I have to do that this week, and I'm a bit stuck.

50
00:03:31,050 --> 00:03:35,010
BETH: Of course. Have you decided yet what to write about?

51
00:03:35,010 --> 00:03:40,534
JAMES: No, I haven't. There's so much that seems interesting -

52
00:03:40,534 --> 00:03:44,400
Viking settlement in other countries, trade, mythology...

53
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:49,732
BETH: Well, what I suggest is that you read an assignment a student wrote last year,

54
00:03:49,732 --> 00:03:51,731
which is kept in the library.

55
00:03:51,731 --> 00:03:55,730
It's short and well focused, and I'm sure you'll find it helpful.

56
00:03:55,730 --> 00:03:58,721
I'll give you the details in a moment.

57
00:03:58,721 --> 00:04:04,330
Textbooks usually cover so many topics, it can be very difficult to choose just one.

58
00:04:04,330 --> 00:04:10,591
JAMES: OK. I've got a DVD of the film about the Vikings that came out earlier this year.

59
00:04:10,591 --> 00:04:12,330
Should I watch that again?

60
00:04:12,330 --> 00:04:16,960
BETH: If it's the one I am thinking of, hmm, I'd ignore it

61
00:04:17,360 --> 00:04:19,680
it's more fantasy than reality.

62
00:04:19,680 --> 00:04:23,764
But I've got a recording of a documentary that you should watch.

63
00:04:23,764 --> 00:04:26,580
It makes some interesting and provocative points

64
00:04:26,580 --> 00:04:29,550
which I think will help you to focus your topic.

65
00:04:29,550 --> 00:04:31,120
JAMES: Right.

66
00:04:31,120 --> 00:04:34,550
JAMES: So then should I work out an outline?

67
00:04:34,550 --> 00:04:38,212
BETH: Yes. Just headings for different sections, at this stage.

68
00:04:38,212 --> 00:04:43,338
And then you should start looking for suitable articles and books to draw on,

69
00:04:43,338 --> 00:04:47,000
and take notes which you organise according to those headings.

70
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:48,580
JAMES: I see.

71
00:04:48,580 --> 00:04:53,490
BETH: Then put short phrases and sentences as bullet points under each heading.

72
00:04:53,490 --> 00:04:57,480
Make sure that this skeleton makes sense and flows properly

73
00:04:57,480 --> 00:04:59,910
before writing up the paper in full.

74
00:04:59,910 --> 00:05:04,440
JAMES: OK. Thanks, that's very helpful.
